      "modelAnswer": "It's like a shopping basket you've been filling at one checkout counter — if you're suddenly sent to a different checkout counter that has no idea what you'd already picked up, your basket looks empty to them even though you never put anything back.\n\n• The classification: this is session state — it belongs to one ongoing series of related operations (building up a cart over the course of a shopping session), not a single operation's private working data, and not a shared, persistent record of the real world accessed across many sessions.\n\n• Why keeping it in one replica's memory causes the bug: session state can be kept client-side, server-side, or both, but here it's kept only server-side, and worse, pinned inside one specific replica's process memory rather than in any store both replicas can read. Because the cart is never shared between replicas, the moment the session migrates to a different process, that replica's memory has no record of it at all.\n\n• A common mistake is: local computation state is private data that's fine to lose after one operation, which isn't true of a multi-step shopping cart; resource state models the real world in a shared, persistent store, which doesn't match a plain in-memory object scoped to one replica; and the network switch is only what triggered the load balancer to route the next request elsewhere — the actual bug is caused by where the cart data was stored, not by the network change itself.\n\nSo the answer is: it's session state, and keeping it only in one replica's memory means a different replica has no way to see it once the session moves."
